 "source_file": "corpus/src/markdown/html_blocks.md",
 "source": "<!-- Raw HTML blocks. The site splices; the formatter keeps the bytes. -->\n\n# Blocks that splice cleanly\n\nA void element on its own line, which is a complete element with no end tag:\n\n<img src=\"diagram.png\" alt=\"A diagram\" width=\"640\">\n\nA balanced block over several lines, with interior indentation prettier does\nnot touch:\n\n<figure class=\"wide\">\n  <img src=\"chart.png\" alt=\"A chart\">\n  <figcaption>Revenue, by quarter</figcaption>\n</figure>\n\nAttribute spelling prettier leaves exactly as written, and so do we:\n\n<div    class=\"loose\"   id=\"spaced\"  data-kept>text</div>\n\nA block a container holds still splices: the item's indentation and the quote\nmarker stay outside the region's extent.\n\n- <div class=\"listed\">a block in a list item</div>\n- plain item\n\n> <div class=\"quoted\">quoted block</div>\n\n# Blocks that do not splice\n\nThe wrapper idiom splits one element across two blocks. The opener carries an\nattribute, so tree-sitter-html accepts it as a complete element and it\nsplices; the closer is an ERROR and stays with the host. Nothing notices that\nthe two belong together, which is why `format = false`:\n\n<div class=\"wrapper\">\n\nMarkdown **inside** the wrapper stays markdown.\n\n</div>\n\nA bare opener carries no attribute, so it is an ERROR on its own and both\nhalves stay with the host:\n\n<section>\n\nStill inside.\n\n</section>\n\n# What never reaches the site\n\nAn inline tag on its own line is a paragraph, not an `html_block`, so the\nsite never sees it and the markdown package emits it verbatim as before:\n\n<span class=\"badge\">inline</span>\n\nQuoting a block needs the blank line above it: a `>` continuation directly\nafter an open html block is an ERROR in the block grammar, so the corpus\ncannot write that form at all.\n",
